## 4. Strongest surviving mathematical statement

Let a named input set be split into nonempty blocks of sizes p and q. Let C_r be all Boolean truth matrices with GF(2) rank at most r across this **fixed** cut. Let kappa(C_r) be the smallest k such that all indexed conditioned counts fixing at most k variables distinguish every pair in C_r. Then

\[
\kappa(C_1)=\max(p,q),\qquad
\kappa(C_r)=p+q\quad(r\geq2).
\]

The rank-one upper bound follows by recovering the nonzero row and column supports of a Boolean rectangle from their marginal sums. The lower bound uses complementary parity on the larger block. The rank-two lower bound uses complementary global parity: the two functions agree on every proper marginal and have rank two across any nontrivial cut. Full assignments give the upper bound.

**Assessment:** CORRECT BUT MINOR; priority of this exact formulation remains uncertain. The proof is short, and the same threshold holds for analogous binary-matrix classes under other rank conventions because rank-one binary matrices are rectangles and the witnesses are unions of two disjoint rectangles. Thus the statement is not evidence of a uniquely GF(2) obstruction.

The exact rank/dimension formula for Q_k, its Fourier equivalence, the high-degree kernel description, the minimum collision distance, and generalized Chow uniqueness all have established antecedents. The theory ledger already acknowledges most of this; that restraint should be preserved. See the full result-by-result audit and sources S1-S5.

## 5. The critical counterexample to the proposed bridge

Consider the known two-element class consisting of global odd and even parity on n variables. Its reconstruction threshold is n: all proper conditioned counts agree. Nevertheless, one bit identifies the member of this class. Given a restriction, the count is half the remaining cube when any variable is free; for a full assignment it is either zero or one.

Therefore

\[
\text{large reconstruction threshold}
\not\Rightarrow
\text{large unrestricted persistent encoding or expensive counting}.
\]

This does not refute the submitted real-linear-sketch theorem: that theorem explicitly ranges over arbitrary real-valued functions and is correct. It refutes a broader interpretation that could otherwise be used to claim that the threshold theorem explains the benchmark losses.

A second distinction concerns updates. From Q_k(f), restricting t variables permits reconstruction of Q_{k-t} of the residual function, for t <= k. It generally does **not** permit a new full Q_k summary with a reset budget. Complementary parity on k+1 variables is an explicit counterexample. A persistence theorem must say which of these contracts it means.

